<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Rxjs on Antomor</title><link>https://antomor.com/tags/rxjs/</link><description>Recent content in Rxjs on Antomor</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en</language><managingEditor>dev@antomor.com (Antonio Morrone)</managingEditor><webMaster>dev@antomor.com (Antonio Morrone)</webMaster><copyright>© 2026 Antonio Morrone</copyright><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Apr 2020 19:40:48 +0200</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://antomor.com/tags/rxjs/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Angular and Rxjs Http Requests: Common scenarios</title><link>https://antomor.com/blog/angular-rxjs-http-requests/</link><pubDate>Thu, 09 Apr 2020 19:40:48 +0200</pubDate><author>dev@antomor.com (Antonio Morrone)</author><guid>https://antomor.com/blog/angular-rxjs-http-requests/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Any modern web application needs soon or later to perform some http request to retrieve data.&#10;Here below I&amp;rsquo;ll describe some common scenario and how to perform such requests using &lt;a href="https://rxjs.dev" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"&gt;RxJS&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;h2 class="relative group"&gt;Single request&#10; &lt;div id="single-request" class="anchor"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10; &#10; &lt;span&#10; class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 -start-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none"&gt;&#10; &lt;a class="text-primary-300 dark:text-neutral-700 !no-underline" href="#single-request" aria-label="Anchor"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#10; &lt;/span&gt;&#10; &#10;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;The most common scenario, no special rxjs handling, since that Angular provides an Http service that returns an &lt;a href="https://rxjs.dev/api/index/class/Observable" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"&gt;Observable&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-typescript" data-lang="typescript"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;getItems&lt;/span&gt;().&lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;subscribe&lt;/span&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Is that easy? Yes. In the particular example, I assumed the http service call was inside the &lt;code&gt;service.getItems&lt;/code&gt; method, but it could be a &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; or anything else returning an observable.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>